  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nPercodan / Codeine / Fosamax / Penicillins\n\nChief Complaint:\npoor PO intake\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n(patient is demented and unable to give history, Hx from son/HCP \nand grandaughter)\n.  \n___ with hx htn, type 2 DM, dementia, CHF, afib, on coumadin,  \nbed- and wheelchair- bound at baseline whi is admitted with \nacute renal failure. \n.\nShe had recent hospitalization ___ for facial cellulitis \nand was discharged on Oral Clindamycin which was planned for a \n___ut since family had trouble giving her Q6h dosing \nwas instructed by PCP to dose Q12h and continue untill tablets \nrun out. Patient is thus still taking clinda. On ___ patient \npresented to OSH with peripheral facial palsey. CT was \nreportedly unremarkable, lyme serology was not checked, no \nsteroids were prescribed. Also was started on Bactrim for UTI \nwhich was reportedly diagnosed per UA w/o symptoms. Unknown if \nculture positive. Since that time patient had difficulty \ndrinking, keeping fluid in mouth. Per family grew progressively \nweak and malaised with increased daytime sleepiness and appeared \ndehydrated. Also note loose watery BM X1 per day for past 3 \ndays. \n.\nIn the ED VS were 97.8 57 106/54 22, 96%. UA was positive for \nketons and concentrated to SG of 1.026 other labs were \nremarkable for Cr/BUN 1.9/34 from ___ at baseline. CXR was \nneg for infiltrate. Received 1L IVF and admitted for acute renal \nfailure and dysphagia. \n.  \nReview of Systems: \nPositive: see HPI. Chronically incontinent of urine. \nNo pain, No SOB, cough, or chest pain. No nausea or change in \nbowel \nhabits. She has chronic ___ dependent edema which is now improved \nsince spends most time in bed.  bilateral L>R leg pain and \nlimited LLE movement d/t pain.  Otherwise negative.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\n___ with hx htn, type 2 DM, dementia, CHF, afib s/p recent \nhospitalization ___ for facial cellulitis, with \n___ development of bell's palsy now admitted for \ndehydration and acute renal failure d/t poor oral intake. \n\n# Poor PO intake and dehydration: per concentrated urine, \nketonuria, renal failure and story of poor PO intake. Patient \nper family had poor PO failure for the past months likely \nworsened now in the context of her recent infection and facial \npalsey. No other CN abnormallities were identified. No hx of \naspiration. Patient was examined by our Speech and Swallow \nservice who recommended continued PO intake with thin liquids \nand ground solids. \n\n# Acute renal failure: Likely secondary to dehydration with a \npossible contribution from taking Bactrim for a urinary tract \ninfection.  She was treated by stopping the Bactrim and starting \nintravenous fluids.  Her creatinine improved and was back to her \nbaseline at the time of discharge.  \n\n# Bell's palsey: Most likely typical idiopathic/viral peripheral \nfacial palsey. Lyme serology is pending. The presentation in the \ncontext of a recent facial eczema with some unilateral erythema \nnow on chin and cheek brings to mind the possibility of HSV \ninfection and Zoster. As above was not able to vizualize \ntympanic membranes. There were no vesicular eruptions on face \nand no eye involvment seen. In view of the quick resultion of \nher rash and > 5 days since presentation anti-viral and/or \nsteroid therapy were not considered to be of potential benefit \nat this point. Artificial tears and eye patch are recommended.  \n\n# Facial cellulitis: This was diagnosed on prior admission \nduring which she was followed by dermatology, dental consult and \nOMFS consult. Feeling was that source was dental. Got panorex \nand was discharge with plan for out patient extraction of #23, \n#24, #25, #26 then reevaluation for consideration of extracting \n#20, #21. She was treated with a course of clindamycine which \nshe completed on the day of her current presentation. Currently \nstill some residual erythema on chin which is resolving, warmth \nand induration are completely resolved, she had no systemic \nsigns of infection. No further Abx were administered during this \nadmission. Outpatient f/u as planned. \n\n# ? recent UTI: was started on Bactrim ___ for UTI which was \nreportedly diagnosed in OSH ED per UA w/o symptoms. Finished ___ \nday course on day of admission. No signs of inflammation or \ninfection on current UA and patient remained asymptomatioc. \nBactrim was held and no further Abx were administered during \nthis admission.   \n\n# loose BM's:  patient reported two episodes of loose BM w/o \nincreased frequency prior to her admission. These have not \nrecurred during her hospital stay. \n\n# ? pancreatic neoplasm: per recent CT ___: Multiple \nsubcentimeter hypodensities in the pancreas could represent \nintraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs). Family and PCP \nare aware. Not likely to be worked per patients age , \ncomorbidities and goals of care. \n\n#  hyperkeratosis of LLQ mouth: seen by OMFS on previous \nadmission - thought to be d/t  mastication, vs burns from hot \ndrink, but malignancy remains a possiblity.\nFollow up with ___ as an outpatient at ___ if \nrelevant per goals of care. \n\n# Hypertension - initially held anti-HTN meds in the context of \nrenal failure.  Lisinopril and nadolol were subsequently \nrestarted at home doses.    \n \n# Hyperlipidemia - continued pravastatin  \n\n# History of DVT, PE, and Factor V Leiden mutation: on \ncoumadine, INR superhteraputic (5.9 on admission, 3.9 at \ndischarge), likely ___ to effect on warfarin of recent abx. \nCoumadin was held and continues to be held at discharge. \nWill check INR on ___ and restart per PCP ___. \n\n# Alzheimer's Dementia - this was stable.   \n\n# Gout  - continued allopurinol  \n\n# Goals of care: Patient with advanced dementia,  declining \nfunction and PO intake for the past few months. She is \ncompletely dependent in ADL's. She is very lovingly cared for by \nher Son and his family. Prognosis is likely no more than ___ \nmonths per her general condition. Family was interested in \nhearing about palliative care options and hospice care. Medical \nteam contacted PCP who was supportive of transition to hospice \ncare. Patient's Son (HCP) and daughter-in-Law met with our \npalliative care consultant who gave guidance regarding goals of \ncare decisions. At this point patient is discharege home with \n___ and family plans to interview out- patient hospices and \ncontinue discussion of transition to hospice-care with patient's \nPCP.  \n\nCode Status: DNR/DNI; confirmed with health care proxy\n\n___, MD pg ___",
